n laws this way she was invited by her  Congress representative Madeline Dean as  a guest at president Trump's State of  the Union address  Jamie hoped the president would tackle  the issue of gun violence in schools she  was left disappointed it was offensive  in a way because he knows it's a problem  there's no reason why we can't reinstate  the assault weapons ban there's been  action before we can take action again  and I think we need to 40,000 people a  year are dying because of something it's  guns and it's time that we tackle it  from a legislative approach we can do  the proactive things with mental health  and we can educate the children and try  to create that change of culture but  that takes time  legislation we can do now in the  meantime Jamie holds her children tight  and hopes that they will never have to  face the turmoil she's experienced over  the past two decades here one two three  one Johnny now from San Diego California  is dr. Reed Malloy he's a forensic  psychologist specializing in psychopathy  mental disorders and targeted violence  and he's worked as a consultant for the  FBI for the past 18 years as well dr.  Malloy thank you very much for joining  us the Columbine school shooting this  was two decades ago and yet its  influence has lasted all the way until  today why is that I think the Columbine  event the mass murder that occurred in  Colorado and now 20 years ago really  became a very momentous event in the  history of criminal in the United States  and has been has fostered a number of  copycat cases since then there have been  upwards of 80 different of individuals  over the past 20 years that  have either attempted to mount an attack  or have succeeded in doing that where  they have said that they were directly  inspired by the events at Columbine it  really became a watershed event in  American criminality you know I was  actually in high school in the US when  the Columbine shooting took place I mean  I remember how how shocking that event  was what effect do you think it's had  perhaps on that generation of students  and also on the country as a whole I  think it is I think it's had a huge  effect on the younger generation and I  think one of the markers for that is  what happened following the Parkland  attacks last year where there is really  an upsurge of of young Americans very  interested in changing the access the  very easy access to high-powered weapons  in the United States which still is a  huge problem but this younger generation  really grew up with Columbine as an  event in the recent history if not prior  to when they were born and in a sense it  normalized that school attacks could  happen anytime anywhere although the  reality is that school attacks are still  a very very infrequent in the United  States despite the press coverage of  such attacks nevertheless of course  these are horrible events and I think  the younger generation as demographics  in the United States shift toward a  younger population we're going to see  quite of substantial changes in various  ways that can keep people safer in the  United States now you mentioned of  course unfortunately since Columbine  there have been dozens more mass school  shootings since that time in 1999 and  and as you said many of these  perpetrators actually did idolize the  shooters that were involved in that  shooting in Columbine now how did these  two students essentially become cult  figures for other gunmen now that's an  excellent question that I don't have an  easy answer for I think part of it was  that what they did was very unique at  the time that they would mount an attack  such as this using both firearms as well  as  tempting to use bombs at the school to  actually create an event that was just  as catastrophic as an event that had  happened four years earlier almost on  the same date which was the Oklahoma  City bombing by Timothy McVeigh on April  19th 1995 and part of their plan was in  a sense to do the Oklahoma City bombing  one better and typically that's what we  see in these young perpetrators is they  will follow very closely individuals  that have preceded them and have carried  out these acts of violence and they both  admire them but then also they want to  do them one better and one of the  dynamics that we're seeing in these  cases is that the way that they do this  one better is to try to have more  casualties as well as to do something  innovative and at the time of Columbine  of both the of the innovation and the  casualties and the location of the  shootings major made a huge impression  it horrified of course most people but  for some particularly adolescents and  young adults who felt very  disenfranchised who felt not a part of  the fabric of their of their school or  their community this became in a sense  and an antihero identification and they  were drawn to these figures wanting to  be like them wanting to make a mark that  otherwise they were not making in their  own lives that footage of of Columbine  of the students streaming out of the  school that was played on repeat on news  stations in the US for hours for four  days now for decades now what about now  in terms of the advent of social media  the invention of live streaming video  has that's now changed things in in  terms of these sorts of mass shootings  yeah I think one of the things that's  done is its accelerated these cases  particularly over the past eight to ten  years and there's good data on that from  both FBI studies as well as from a study  that Harvard Public Health did  concerning the this uptick in numbers of  cases so social media really in a sense  becomes an accelerant it becomes a  vector that can carry this in fact  this contagion into other into other  young minds and I think that there also  is now a much greater awareness of the  impact of social media and the  commercial media coverage of these  events and there's been we think  substantial changes in that by the media  for instance the the photo of the  perpetrator is not published as widely  as they used to be they're not they're  not shown in some element where they're  posing with their weapons or with some  kind of camouflage outfit that sort of  glorifies they're a pseudo commando a  presentation and then all three not  clean the loops of the video loops  played over and over again one of the  striking examples of this is what  happened in Christchurch New Zealand  just within the past month we did not  see in the worldwide commercial media  the videotaping the live-streaming of  the massacre that had occurred then and  you see actually you saw a very muted  response to the perpetrator himself and  so I think we're saying some seeing some  important changes dr. Reid Malloy  forensic psychologist specializing in  mental disorders and targeted violence  thank you so much for joining us thank  you thank you very much
 

